WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.186
In electrolysis, the electrons that flow through the wire are the ones that make the product. Count the electrons, and you know how much product you made.

00:00:11.686 --> 00:00:14.256
Every Faraday calculation follows the same chain.

00:00:14.856 --> 00:00:25.990
Current and time give the charge. Charge gives moles of electrons. The half-reaction gives the ratio. Then moles of product, and finally the mass or the volume.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:33.858
A current of two amperes flows for one thousand nine hundred and thirty seconds, depositing copper.

00:00:34.458 --> 00:00:42.164
The charge is current times time. Two times one thousand nine hundred and thirty gives three thousand eight hundred and sixty coulombs.

00:00:42.764 --> 00:00:49.489
The time must be in seconds. Minutes times sixty. Hours times three thousand six hundred.

00:00:51.989 --> 00:00:59.562
One mole of electrons carries ninety-six thousand five hundred coulombs. That is the Faraday constant, on your data sheet.

00:01:00.162 --> 00:01:07.058
Three thousand eight hundred and sixty divided by ninety-six thousand five hundred gives zero comma zero four moles of electrons.

00:01:09.558 --> 00:01:20.656
The half-reaction is copper two plus, plus two electrons, gives copper. How many moles of copper do zero comma zero four moles of electrons deposit? Decide now.

00:01:25.256 --> 00:01:33.302
Zero comma zero two moles. Each copper atom needs two electrons, so the moles of copper are half the moles of electrons.

00:01:35.802 --> 00:01:43.782
Mass is moles times molar mass. Zero comma zero two times sixty-three comma five gives one comma two seven grams of copper.

00:01:46.282 --> 00:02:01.735
The most common mistake is to treat moles of electrons as moles of product. The examiners' report for twenty twenty-five said many candidates are still neglecting the ratio in the half-reaction. Always write the half-reaction first. It gives you the ratio.

00:02:04.235 --> 00:02:24.095
You can run the chain backwards too. To deposit fifteen comma nine grams of copper with a current of twenty amperes: find the moles of copper, double them for the electrons, multiply by the Faraday constant to get the charge, and divide by the current. It takes about two thousand four hundred and sixteen seconds, roughly forty minutes.

00:02:26.595 --> 00:02:34.876
Open the Faraday machine. Change the current and the time, switch to two cells in series, and watch both cells share the same charge.
